WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Nature and Use of Performance Baselines

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.633
Welcome to this session on baselines.

3
00:00:08.633 --> 00:00:12.633
Very interesting. You'll recall, we talked about the role of measurement.

4
00:00:12.633 --> 00:00:17.199
Now, we’re going to talk about the idea of a baseline. A numerical baseline.

5
00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:20.033
And to begin, it may seem kind of funny a first way to start this,

6
00:00:20.033 --> 00:00:25.999
but we are going to start with the idea of a need. What is a need?

7
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:33.033
Well let’s take for example some scale of measurement. Some quantity called Y.

8
00:00:33.033 --> 00:00:38.533
An outcome. Remember, that Y function of X.

9
00:00:38.533 --> 00:00:42.799
What we’re looking at, is the scale of Y.

10
00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:49.766
And on that scale, what we’re going to say is that this is the is condition.

11
00:00:49.766 --> 00:00:56.766
This is where we currently are assessed. That's our baseline.

12
00:00:56.766 --> 00:01:02.232
Kind of like that is condition.

13
00:01:02.233 --> 00:01:08.199
So, maybe this over here is where we

14
00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:14.600
ought to be. That's the goal.

15
00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:21.066
So, we have a baseline and we have a goal. The difference between

16
00:01:21.066 --> 00:01:28.032
the ought and the is, or  the difference between the baseline and the goal can be symbolized

17
00:01:28.033 --> 00:01:35.066
by the Greek symbol Delta. Which means change. Which means difference, if you will.

18
00:01:35.066 --> 00:01:39.332
That is called need.

19
00:01:39.333 --> 00:01:44.299
Need is merely a gap.

20
00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:49.433
It's a gap between what we perceive or measure of what is,

21
00:01:49.433 --> 00:01:53.033
and what we believe ought to be.

22
00:01:53.033 --> 00:02:00.033
So, when we measure it. We make that verifiable and repeatable and confirmable.

23
00:02:00.033 --> 00:02:03.999
Between the is and the ought, so that we can quantify the need.

24
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:06.900
In this case, like a customer's need.

25
00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:10.566
The customer has a certain performance characteristic

26
00:02:10.566 --> 00:02:15.066
called Y out there. Whether it's a commercial Y or an industrial Y.

27
00:02:15.066 --> 00:02:20.332
Y could be in the commercial world the amount of time it takes to resolve a customer complaint.

28
00:02:20.333 --> 00:02:24.233
Y could be the temperature of the solder.

29
00:02:24.233 --> 00:02:29.899
Y can be anything that the customer wants. That the customer must have.

30
00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:34.133
So this is like a specification. It's a standard.

31
00:02:34.133 --> 00:02:39.599
It's what the customer expects. It's an expectation.

32
00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:43.600
It's a goal. It’s what ought to be.

33
00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:48.700
And over here, off course, we have coming up. What is.

34
00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:55.000
It’s what is and what ought. And the difference is the gap. That's a need.

35
00:02:55.000 --> 00:03:02.000
Now, what's critical to defining need, you can see, is two things. Absolutely critical.

36
00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:08.333
You have to have a baseline and you have to have a goal. An expectation.

37
00:03:08.333 --> 00:03:14.299
If you don't have a baseline and you don't have a goal, how can you have a need?

38
00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:19.500
The gap would be undefined. And therefore, the need is undefined.

39
00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:24.033
So, you can start to see now, that all of this stuff begins to blend together.

40
00:03:24.033 --> 00:03:28.033
The idea of a baseline, the idea of a need, and gap.

41
00:03:28.033 --> 00:03:34.833
The idea that Y is a function of X. The idea of specifications. The idea of baselining.

42
00:03:34.833 --> 00:03:40.199
The idea of Six Sigma. All this begins to blend together over time,

43
00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:46.266
into an operative philosophy. A system by which we manage the business

44
00:03:46.266 --> 00:03:51.066
and look at life and look at our processes and our customers.

45
00:03:51.066 --> 00:03:57.099
And gauge how we get better at doing things. Again, this is all dependent upon measurement,

46
00:03:57.100 --> 00:04:01.966
and Y is a function of X, and our ability to find baselines, and so on.

47
00:04:01.966 --> 00:04:07.899
This is a way we set out roadmaps of how to get better. Absolutely essential.

48
00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:12.900
Thank you for choosing SSMI.
